Implementation follow-up, 2026-09-20:
The CLI/IR/native reduction was RED with zero Rust compile errors, then GREEN.
Both gcc-14 and clang-19 assemble/link the subject's exact same assembly;
both native executions exit 0. ELF inspection verifies four link-ordered
WAL table entries, first-NOP relocation addresses, all N/M instruction bytes,
no entry for notrace_entry, and aligned_entry's callable address divisible
by 64. The latter deliberately follows EvoIR Section 5.2, not the conflicting
oracle combined-alignment behavior above. CFI starts at the callable symbol;
the post-symbol NOPs precede PAC and frame setup.
The 23 command-projection tests pass; -fpatchable-function-entry options now
reach EvoCC in written order rather than being omitted. The 13 independent
proof tests pass, including patch size/offset/removal mutations even with a
recomputed output digest. This is targeted closure, not a new kernel boot.

Additional fixed-image declaration probes (gnu11, O0, -S, source on stdin):
  A: attribute(3,1) declaration, followed by unmarked definition:
     GCC and Clang both emit (3,1).
  B: attribute(3,1) declaration, followed by attribute(4,2) definition:
     GCC and Clang both emit (4,2).
  C: attribute(3,1) definition, followed by attribute(4,2) declaration:
     GCC emits (4,2), Clang (3,1); EvoCC rejects E2943.
  D: attribute(1,2) definition: GCC warns, Clang rejects; EvoCC rejects E2903.
Here attribute means __attribute__((patchable_function_entry(N,M))); all
function declarations are int f(void), and all bodies are {return 0;}.

